#doc (Manual) "Milnor K-theory" =>
%%%
tag := "sec:K"
%%%

Theorems B, C and D follow from {cref "thm:A"}[] together with one family of homomorphisms out
of $`\Kr_2`, the tame symbols.
In this chapter $`K` and $`L` are perfect fields.
We write $`L^\times_\QQ = L^\times \otimes \QQ = \Kr_1(L)` and denote the product in $`\Kr_*` by
$`\{\cdot, \cdot\}`.
Since $`\Kr_*(L)` is the quotient of the tensor algebra of $`L^\times_\QQ` by the ideal generated
by the elements $`[a] \otimes [1-a]`, a $`\QQ`-linear map $`B` from
$`L^\times_\QQ \otimes L^\times_\QQ` to a vector space factors through $`\Kr_2(L)` if and only if
$`B([a], [1-a]) = 0` for all $`a \in L \sm \{0, 1\}`.

All results of this chapter are formalized.
The proofs of Theorems B, C and D use {cref "thm:A"}[] only through its formal statements
{name}`theoremA_perfect` and {name}`theoremA_perfect_unique`.

# The rational Milnor ring
%%%
tag := "ss:milnorRing"
number := false
%%%

The formalization defines $`\Kr_*(L)` and $`\Kr_*(L|l)` in one go, by generators and relations
over the group $`(L^\times/H) \otimes \QQ` = {name}`VOf` `H` of
{ref "ss:V"}[Section 2.3], for a subgroup $`H \subset L^\times`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ}

For $`H = 1` this is the blueprint's $`\Kr_*(L)`: tensoring with $`\QQ` commutes with forming the
tensor algebra and the quotient, so $`\QQ \otimes \KM_*(L)` is the tensor algebra over $`\QQ` of
$`L^\times_\QQ` modulo the Steinberg elements.
For $`H = l^\times` the blueprint defines $`\Kr_*(L|l)` as the quotient of $`\Kr_*(L)` by the
ideal generated by $`l^\times \subset \Kr_1(L)`; dividing the tensor algebra of $`L^\times_\QQ` by
the ideal generated by the image of $`l^\times` gives the tensor algebra of
$`V_{L|l} = L^\times_\QQ / l^\times_\QQ`, and the Steinberg elements map to Steinberg elements, so
the two definitions agree.
This comparison is not formalized, and nothing depends on it: Theorems B and C are stated for the
rings as defined here.
The Steinberg relations are indexed by pairs of units $`a, b` with $`a + b = 1`, so that no junk
value of $`1 - a` occurs.

The ring is generated by the image of `ι : VOf H → MilnorKQ H`, and has the expected universal
property ({name}`MilnorKQ.lift`): a $`\QQ`-linear map $`f` from `VOf H` to a
$`\QQ`-algebra with $`f[a]\,f[b] = 0` whenever $`a + b = 1` extends uniquely to an algebra
homomorphism ({name}`MilnorKQ.algHom_ext`).

_Grading._
The degree $`n` part is the $`n`-th power of the image of `ι`:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.grade}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.gradedAlgebra}

The proof follows Mathlib's grading of the exterior algebra: the Steinberg relations are
homogeneous, so `ι` followed by the inclusion of degree $`1` into the direct sum
$`\bigoplus_n \Kr_n` lifts to an inverse of the canonical map.
The degree $`0` part is $`\QQ` ({name}`MilnorKQ.grade_zero`) and the degree $`1` part
is `VOf H`, embedded by `ι` ({name}`MilnorKQ.grade_one`,
{name}`MilnorKQ.ι_injective`); injectivity is proved with the projection to degree
one, which does not need the direct sum decomposition:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.π₁}

_Homomorphisms of graded algebras._
A homomorphism of graded algebras is an algebra homomorphism that preserves degrees, and its
degree one part is the blueprint's $`\varphi_1`:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.IsGraded}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.degOne}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.IsGraded.apply_ι}

Since the ring is generated in degree one, a graded homomorphism is determined by its degree one
part ({name}`MilnorKQ.IsGraded.ext`).

_Functoriality and_ $`r^{\deg}`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.map}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.degScale}

These satisfy $`(\sigma \circ \tau)_* = \sigma_* \circ \tau_*`
({name}`MilnorKQ.map_comp`), $`r^{\deg}` is multiplication by $`r^n` in degree
$`n` ({name}`MilnorKQ.degScale_apply_of_mem_grade`) and an automorphism for
$`r \neq 0` ({name}`MilnorKQ.degScaleEquiv`).
For subgroups $`H \subset H'` there is a projection {name}`MilnorKQ.proj`; for
instance $`\Kr_*(L) \to \Kr_*(L|l)`.

_Bilinear maps out of_ $`\Kr_2`.
The factorization criterion stated at the beginning of the chapter is formalized for maps
defined on the whole ring, which avoids the direct sum decomposition:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.ofBilin}

It is constructed by lifting $`\alpha \mapsto` (left multiplication by $`(0, \alpha, 0)`) to an
algebra homomorphism into the endomorphisms of $`\QQ \times V \times W`, with the product
$`(q, x, w)(q', x', w') = (qq', qx' + q'x, qw' + q'w + B(x, x'))`, and taking the third component
of the image of $`(1, 0, 0)`.

_Purely inseparable extensions._
The blueprint's remark that $`\Kr_*` does not change under purely inseparable extensions is used in
the proof of Theorem D.
For a $`p`-radical extension $`M|K` the map $`K^\times_\QQ \to M^\times_\QQ` is bijective
({name}`VOf.mapRingHom_algebraMap_bijective`), and:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.perfectClosureEquiv}

# A tame symbol
%%%
tag := "ss:tame"
%%%

:::lem "7.1" (tag := "lem:tame")
Let $`v` be a valuation of $`L` with valuation ring $`O`, residue field $`\kappa` and value
group $`\Gamma_v`, and put $`\Gamma_{v,\QQ} = \Gamma_v \otimes \QQ`,
$`\kappa^\times_\QQ = \kappa^\times \otimes \QQ`.
There is a $`\QQ`-linear map
$$`T_v \colon \Kr_2(L) \to \Gamma_{v,\QQ} \otimes_\QQ \kappa^\times_\QQ
  \qquad\text{with}\qquad T_v\{c, b\} = -v(b) \otimes [\bar c]`
for all $`c \in O^\times` and $`b \in L^\times`.
:::

In Lean a valuation is a {name}`ValuationSubring` `A` of $`L`, with residue field
`IsLocalRing.ResidueField A`.
The value group is taken to be $`L^\times / O^\times` (Mathlib's `A.unitGroup` is $`O^\times`
as a subgroup of $`L^\times`), so $`\Gamma_{v,\QQ}` = `VOf A.unitGroup` and
$`\kappa^\times_\QQ` = `VOf (⊥ : Subgroup κˣ)`.

_Proof._
The valuation induces a surjection $`v \colon L^\times_\QQ \to \Gamma_{v,\QQ}`
({name}`Tame.val`, the projection `VOf ⊥ → VOf A.unitGroup`).
Its kernel $`U` is the image of $`O^\times \otimes \QQ`, which injects into $`L^\times_\QQ`
because $`\QQ` is flat over $`\ZZ` ({name}`Tame.unitsIncl_injective`), and the
residue map induces $`\mathrm{res} \colon U \to \kappa^\times_\QQ`.
Choose a $`\QQ`-linear retraction $`\pi \colon L^\times_\QQ \to U` of the inclusion and put
$`\mathfrak u = \mathrm{res} \circ \pi`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.Tame.unitPart}

Define
$$`B(\alpha, \beta) = v(\alpha) \otimes \mathfrak u(\beta) - v(\beta) \otimes \mathfrak u(\alpha).`
This is bilinear and alternating, and has the stated value on $`(c, b)` with $`c \in O^\times`,
since then $`v(c) = 0` and $`\mathfrak u([c]) = [\bar c]`.
It remains to check $`B([a], [1-a]) = 0` for $`a \in L \sm \{0, 1\}`.
If $`v(a) > 0`, then $`1 - a` is a unit with residue $`1`, so $`v(1-a) = 0` and
$`\mathfrak u([1-a]) = 0` ({name}`Tame.val_unitPart_cls_one_sub`).
If $`v(a) < 0`, then $`[1-a] = [a] + [1 - a^{-1}]` in $`L^\times_\QQ` and $`1 - a^{-1}` is a unit
with residue $`1`; hence $`v(1-a) = v(a)`, $`\mathfrak u([1-a]) = \mathfrak u([a])` and $`B = 0`
({name}`Tame.val_unitPart_cls_of_one_lt`).
If $`v(a) = 0` and $`v(1-a) = 0`, both terms vanish.
If $`v(a) = 0` and $`v(1-a) > 0`, the first case applied to $`1 - a` gives
$`B([1-a], [a]) = 0`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.Tame.form_cls_cls_eq_zero}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.tameSymbol}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.tameSymbol_symbol_of_mem}

The tame symbols are applied to valuations provided by the following standard fact: if
$`M \subset L` is a subfield and $`b \in L` is transcendental over $`M`, there is a valuation
$`v` of $`L` which is trivial on $`M` and satisfies $`v(b) > 0`; its residue field contains
$`M`.
The blueprint extends the $`b`-adic valuation of $`M(b)` to $`L`; the formal proof takes a
valuation subring dominating the subring $`M[b]`, with $`b` among its non-units (Chevalley's
extension theorem in Mathlib).
Since Mathlib's valuations are written multiplicatively, $`v(b) > 0` becomes
`A.valuation b < 1`:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.exists_valuationSubring_of_transcendental}

A valuation trivial on $`M` is trivial on the elements of $`L` algebraic over $`M`
({name}`ValuationExt.mem_of_isAlgebraic`), $`M` embeds in the residue field
({name}`ValuationExt.residueHom`), and the residue map is injective on the elements
algebraic over $`M`:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.ValuationExt.eq_of_residue_eq_of_isAlgebraic}

Consequently, if $`c` is algebraic over $`M`, then $`\mathfrak u[c] = 0` only if $`c` is a root of
unity:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.Tame.unitPart_cls_eq_zero_iff_of_isAlgebraic}

Because $`\Kr_*(L|l)` is defined directly ({ref "ss:milnorRing"}[above]) rather than as a
quotient of $`\Kr_*(L)`, part (a) of the next lemma uses a relative tame symbol, built in the same
way from the bilinear form $`B'(\alpha, \beta) = v(\alpha) \otimes \mathfrak u'(\beta) -
v(\beta) \otimes \mathfrak u'(\alpha)` on $`V_{L|l}`, where $`\mathfrak u'` is $`\mathfrak u`
followed by the projection to $`(\kappa^\times / l^\times)_\QQ`:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.tameSymbolRel}

:::lem "7.2" (tag := "lem:symbols")
Let $`l \subset L` be relatively algebraically closed.

* *(a)* If $`a, b \in L` are algebraically independent over $`l`, then $`\{a, b\} \neq 0` in
  $`\Kr_2(L|l)`.
* *(b)* If $`c \in L^\times` is algebraic over the prime field and not a root of unity, and
  $`b \in L` is transcendental over the prime field, then $`\{c, b\} \neq 0` in $`\Kr_2(L)`.
:::

_Proof._
(a) Let $`M = l(a)` and let $`v` be a valuation trivial on $`M` with $`v(b) > 0`.
Then $`T'_v\{a, b\} = -v(b) \otimes [\bar a]`.
Here $`v(b) \neq 0` in $`\Gamma_{v,\QQ}`, because the value group is torsion free, and
$`[\bar a] \neq 0` in $`(\kappa^\times / l^\times)_\QQ`: if $`\bar a^n = \bar u` with
$`u \in l^\times` and $`n \geq 1`, then $`a^n = u` because the residue map is injective on $`M`,
and $`a` would be algebraic over $`l` ({name}`Tame.unitPartRel_cls_ne_zero`).
A tensor product of non-zero vectors of $`\QQ`-vector spaces is non-zero
({name}`tmul_ne_zero`).
(b) Let $`v` be a valuation trivial on the prime field with $`v(b) > 0`.
Then $`T_v\{c, b\} = -v(b) \otimes [\bar c]`, and $`[\bar c] \neq 0` because $`c` is not a root
of unity.

The blueprint takes for $`M` the relative algebraic closure of $`l(a)` in $`L`; the field $`l(a)`
suffices, because the residue map is injective on every field on which the valuation is
trivial.
As a result, (a) does not need $`l` to be relatively algebraically closed, and the formal
statement does not assume it.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.symbol_ne_zero_of_algebraicIndependent}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.symbol_ne_zero_of_isAlgebraic_of_transcendental}

In terms of $`V_{L|l} = \Kr_1(L|l)`, part (a) says: if $`v_1, v_2 \in V_{L|l}` satisfy
$`\{v_1, v_2\} = 0` in $`\Kr_2(L|l)`, then $`v_1, v_2` are algebraically dependent or one of them
is $`0`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.not_vAlgIndep_of_ι_mul_ι_eq_zero}

# Proof of Theorem B
%%%
tag := "ss:proofB"
%%%

Let $`\psi = \varphi_1 \circ [\,\cdot\,] \colon K^\times \to V_{L|l}`.
Since $`\varphi_1` is injective and $`k` is relatively algebraically closed,
$`\ker \psi = k^\times` by (V1).
For $`x \in K \sm k` we have $`\{\psi(x), \psi(1-x)\} = \varphi_2\{x, 1-x\} = 0`, so
$`\psi(x), \psi(1-x)` are algebraically dependent by {cref "lem:symbols"}[] (a):

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.not_vAlgIndep_of_isGraded}

Thus (A1)–(A4) hold, and {cref "thm:A"}[] gives $`\tau` and $`r` with $`\varphi_1 = r\, \tau_*`
in degree $`1`.
As both $`\varphi` and $`r^{\deg} \circ \tau_*` are homomorphisms of algebras generated in degree
$`1`, they agree:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.eq_degScale_comp_map}

Uniqueness ({name}`theoremB_unique`) is the uniqueness in Theorem A, applied to
$`\psi = r[\tau]`, which is non-zero because $`k \neq K` and $`\tau^{-1}(l) = k`;
$`r' \neq 0` need not be assumed. ∎

# Proof of Theorem C
%%%
tag := "ss:proofC"
%%%

Let $`\psi` be the composition of $`\varphi_1 \circ [\,\cdot\,] \colon K^\times \to L^\times_\QQ`
with the projection to $`V = V_{L|l_0}`, and let $`C_L \subset L^\times_\QQ` be the kernel of this
projection, the image of $`l_0^\times \otimes \QQ`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremC.psi}

Put $`k' = \{0\} \cup \ker \psi`.
As in the proof of Theorem B, {cref "lem:symbols"}[] (a) gives:

* *(eq:dep)* for every $`x \in K \sm \{0, 1\}`, the elements $`\psi(x)` and $`\psi(1-x)` of $`V`
  are algebraically dependent, or one of them is $`0`.

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremC.not_vAlgIndep_psi}

:::lem "7.3" (tag := "lem:kprime")
$`k'` is a subfield of $`K`.
:::

_Proof._
$`\ker \psi` is a subgroup containing $`-1`, so it suffices to show: if $`z \in \ker \psi` and
$`z \neq 1`, then $`1 - z \in \ker \psi` ({name}`subfieldOfKer`).

(i) _$`z` is not a root of unity._
Then $`\varphi_1[z]` is a non-zero element of $`C_L` (by injectivity of $`\varphi_1`, since
$`[z] \neq 0` in $`K^\times_\QQ`), say $`\varphi_1[z] = \frac1N [c]` with $`c \in l_0` not a root of
unity.
Write $`\varphi_1[1-z] = \frac1{N'}[b]`.
Then $`\{c, b\} = NN' \varphi_2\{z, 1-z\} = 0`, so $`b` is algebraic over the prime field by
{cref "lem:symbols"}[] (b), i.e. $`1 - z \in \ker \psi`
({name}`TheoremC.psi_eq_zero_of_not_root`).

(ii) _$`z = \zeta` is a root of unity and $`\chr K > 0`._
Then $`1 - \zeta` is algebraic over a finite field, hence a root of unity, and
$`\varphi_1[1-\zeta] = 0` ({name}`TheoremC.psi_eq_zero_of_ringChar_ne_zero`; the
formal proof uses that the prime field, hence $`\FF_p(1 - \zeta)`, is finite).

(iii) _$`z = \zeta` is a root of unity and $`\chr K = 0`._
Then $`\chr L = 0` by (A4).
Let $`\Delta = \dlog \circ \psi \colon K^\times \to \Omega_{L|l_0}`
({name}`TheoremC.delta`), where $`\dlog` is the injective map of
{ref "ss:model0"}[Section 6.3.1]; so $`\ker \Delta = \ker \psi`.
By (S2), algebraically dependent elements of $`V` have proportional images under $`\dlog`, and
algebraically independent ones have linearly independent images.
Suppose $`c = 1 - \zeta \notin \ker \psi`, so $`\Delta(c) \neq 0`.
There is $`x \in K`, transcendental over $`\QQ`, with $`\Delta(x) \notin L\, \Delta(c)`
({name}`TheoremC.exists_transcendental_delta_not_mem`): by (A3) there is $`x_1` with
$`\Delta(x_1) \notin L\Delta(c)`; if $`x_1` is algebraic, take $`z_0 \in K \sm k_0`, and one of
$`z_0`, $`z_0 x_1` works.
Fix such an $`x`; then $`x` and $`cx` are not in $`\ker \psi`.
By (eq:dep), $`\Delta(1-x) = \mu\Delta(x)` and $`\Delta(1-cx) = \mu'\Delta(cx)` with
$`\mu, \mu' \in L`.
Let $`u = c(1-x)/(c-1)`.
Since $`c - 1 = -\zeta` and $`1 - u = (1 - cx)/\zeta`,
$$`\Delta(u) = \Delta(c) + \mu\, \Delta(x) \neq 0, \qquad
  \Delta(1-u) = \Delta(1-cx) = \mu'\bigl(\Delta(c) + \Delta(x)\bigr),`
and these are proportional by (eq:dep).
Hence $`\mu' = 0` or $`\mu = 1`.
If $`\mu' = 0`, then $`1 - cx \in \ker \psi`.
It cannot be a non-root of unity, since then (i) would give $`cx = 1 - (1 - cx) \in \ker \psi`.
So $`1 - cx` is a root of unity and $`x` is algebraic over $`\QQ`.
If $`\mu = 1`, then $`\Delta((1-x)/x) = 0`, so $`1 - 1/x \in \ker \psi`; by (i) it is a root of
unity, as otherwise $`1/x \in \ker \psi`.
Again $`x` is algebraic over $`\QQ`.
Both cases contradict the choice of $`x`.
Hence $`1 - \zeta \in \ker \psi` ({name}`TheoremC.psi_eq_zero_of_root`).

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremC.psi_eq_zero_of_add_eq_one}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.subfieldOfKer}

By {cref "lem:kprime"}[] and (eq:dep), the homomorphism $`\psi \colon K^\times \to V_{L|l_0}`
satisfies (A1) and (A2) with $`k = k'`; (A3) and (A4) hold by assumption.
{cref "thm:A"}[] gives a field embedding $`\tau \colon K \to L` and $`r \in \QQ^\times` with
$`\psi = r[\tau]` in $`V_{L|l_0}`.
An element of $`K` is algebraic over the prime field if and only if its image under $`\tau` is,
because $`\tau` restricts to an isomorphism of prime fields ({name}`botEquiv`,
{name}`isAlgebraic_bot_map_iff`); so $`\tau^{-1}(l_0) = k_0`.
(The blueprint concludes $`k' = k_0` from this; the formal proof does not need $`k' = k_0`.)

It remains to show that $`\varphi_1[x] = r[\tau(x)]` holds in $`L^\times_\QQ`, not only modulo
$`C_L`.
Let $`\chi(x) = \varphi_1[x] - r[\tau(x)] \in C_L`; this is a homomorphism $`K^\times \to C_L`.
If $`\chr L > 0` then $`C_L = 0`, because every non-zero element of $`l_0` is a root of unity:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremC.proj_injective_of_ringChar_ne_zero}

Let $`\chr L = 0` and $`x \in K \sm k_0`, so $`s = \tau(x)` is transcendental over $`l_0`.
Choose a valuation $`v` of $`L`, trivial on $`l_0`, with $`v(s) > 0`.
Then $`v(1-s) = 0`, $`\mathfrak u[1 - s] = 0`, and $`v` vanishes on $`C_L`.
Expanding $`0 = T_v\, \varphi_2\{x, 1-x\} = T_v\{r[s] + \chi(x),\, r[1-s] + \chi(1-x)\}` with the
formula $`T_v(\alpha\beta) = v(\alpha) \otimes \mathfrak u(\beta) - v(\beta) \otimes
\mathfrak u(\alpha)`, all terms vanish except $`r\, v(s) \otimes \mathfrak u(\chi(1-x))`.
Since $`v(s) \neq 0` and $`\mathfrak u` is injective on $`C_L` (an element $`\frac1N[c]` of
$`C_L` with $`\mathfrak u[c] = 0` has $`c` a root of unity), $`\chi(1-x) = 0` for all
$`x \in K \sm k_0`.
So $`\chi` vanishes on $`K \sm k_0`, and then also on $`k_0^\times` (write $`y = (y z_0)/z_0`
with $`z_0 \notin k_0`).

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremC.eq_zero_of_tameSymbol}

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremC.degOne_cls_eq_of_proj}

So $`\varphi_1 = r\, \tau_*`, and $`\varphi = r^{\deg} \circ \tau_*` as in the proof of
Theorem B.
Uniqueness ({name}`theoremC_unique`) follows from the uniqueness in Theorem A,
applied to $`K^\times \to V_{L|l_0}`. ∎

# Proof of Theorem D
%%%
tag := "ss:proofD"
%%%

We may replace $`K, L` by their perfect closures ({name}`MilnorKQ.perfectClosureEquiv`).
Let $`\varphi` be an isomorphism.
Its inverse is graded too, since $`\Kr_*` is the direct sum of its homogeneous parts:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.IsGraded.symm}

So the degree one part of $`\varphi` is bijective:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.MilnorKQ.degOne_bijective}

Both $`\varphi_1` and $`\varphi_1^{-1}` are surjective, so (A3) holds for $`\varphi` and for
$`\varphi^{-1}` with $`d = 3`, and with $`d = 2` for the one whose target has characteristic $`0`:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremD.exists_eq_of_degOne_bijective}

If $`\chr K = 0 < \chr L`, {cref "thm:C"}[] applies to $`\varphi^{-1}` and gives an embedding
$`L \to K`, which is absurd ({name}`ringChar_eq_of_ringHom`); similarly
$`\chr L = 0 < \chr K` is impossible.
In particular, if one of the fields has characteristic $`0` so does the other.
So (A4) holds in both directions, and {cref "thm:C"}[] gives embeddings $`\tau \colon K \to L`,
$`\tau' \colon L \to K` and $`r, r' \in \QQ^\times` with $`\varphi = r^{\deg}\tau_*` and
$`\varphi^{-1} = r'^{\deg}\tau'_*`.
Then $`rr'[\tau\tau'(y)] = [y]` in $`L^\times_\QQ` for all $`y`
({name}`TheoremD.smul_cls_comp_eq`), and the uniqueness in {cref "thm:A"}[], applied
to the canonical map $`L^\times \to V_{L|l_0}`, shows that $`\tau\tau'` is a power of Frobenius:

{docstring MilnorAlgDep.TheoremD.bijective_of_smul_cls_map_eq}

So $`\tau\tau'` is bijective, hence $`\tau` is surjective; being a field homomorphism it is
injective, so $`\tau` is an isomorphism.
The blueprint also shows that $`\tau'\tau` is a power of Frobenius; this is not needed. ∎
